{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically above realized prices across most horizons; apply a downward level shift (stronger when recent history is falling).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model underestimates downside momentum after a peak and keeps prices too high for too long; increase responsiveness to recent negative returns (e.g., overweight last 5–10 days).",
    "Insufficient mean-reversion in downtrends: forecasts drift back up toward mid/high-70s even when the market keeps selling off; suppress mean-reversion signals when short-term trend is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: overprediction grows with lead time (largest in weeks 2–4); use a horizon-scaled correction where the downward adjustment increases with forecast step.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: model projects stronger/longer rebounds than realized following drops; cap rebound magnitude and duration unless confirmed by multiple consecutive up days in history."
  ]
}
